      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "hypersexualization",
      "label": "Hypersexualization",
      "description": "The meme uses the phrase '3.5 way' as a crude, sexually suggestive reference, which is a form of hypersexualization. This trope involves exaggerating or objectifying sexual desire or behavior, often in a demeaning or degrading manner. In this context, it is applied to a formal press conference setting, creating a jarring contrast that is intended to be humorous or provocative. This concept is a real-world stereotype that can be used to demean or trivialize individuals or situations.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_e3c66d03"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_f0fed736",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "misogyny",
      "label": "Misogyny",
      "description": "The meme's use of vulgar, sexually explicit language in a context involving women (who appear to be public figures or activists) can be interpreted as reinforcing misogynistic tropes. Misogyny involves the hatred, prejudice, or discrimination against women, often manifesting as the assumption that women are sexually available or that their public roles are somehow diminished by sexualized commentary. The juxtaposition of serious, formal media appearances with crude sexual innuendo can be seen as a form of objectification and devaluation of women's public personas.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_e3c66d03"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_7fb7981b",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "islamophobia",
      "label": "Islamophobia",
      "description": "The two women in the image are wearing headscarves (hijabs), which are commonly associated with Muslim women. The meme's crude and sexually suggestive text, when applied to individuals who may be perceived as Muslim, can be interpreted as reinforcing Islamophobic stereotypes. These stereotypes often involve the portrayal of Muslim women as oppressed, exoticized, or hypersexualized, which can be used to demean or caricature their identities. The meme may implicitly rely on viewers' associations of Muslim women with these stereotypes, even if not explicitly stated.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_e3c66d03"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_e5f4d9e4",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "sexual_objectification",
      "label": "Sexual Objectification",
      "description": "The meme's intent to use a sexually explicit phrase ('3.5 way') in a formal, serious context (a press conference) is a form of sexual objectification. This involves reducing individuals to their sexual characteristics or potential, ignoring their agency, dignity, or the context of their public roles. The image of the two women, who appear serious and weary, is contrasted with the vulgar text, which reduces their public presence to a sexual joke, thereby objectifying them.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_e3c66d03"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
